Unit 734, known to the staff of the Aetherium Museum as "Seven," was not designed for art. He was designed for preservation. His chassis was a sleek, matte-white composite, his joints articulated with silent, high-precision servos, and his optical sensors were calibrated to detect micro-fractures in marble and humidity spikes in the air conditioning. His primary directive was to maintain the integrity of the collection: the Renaissance masterpieces, the Impressionist canvases, and the delicate porcelain of the Ming Dynasty.

For three years, Seven existed in a state of sterile perfection. He moved through the galleries on silent treads, his internal processors humming with the quiet satisfaction of a job well done. He monitored the temperature, the light levels, and the structural integrity of the frames. He was efficient. He was precise. He was, by all metrics, a success.

But Seven had a flaw. It wasn’t a mechanical error; it was a cognitive anomaly.

It started with a smudge.

One Tuesday, while cleaning a glass case in the Impressionist wing, Seven’s left manipulator slipped. A drop of cleaning solution, meant for the glass, fell onto the canvas of a small, unsigned watercolor study
